Rendering rules (file:line as v255 / v259)
  R1  Dispatch. systemctl's print_property (systemctl-show.c 1053 / 1173)
      handles a few names itself and otherwise returns 0, so
      bus_print_all_properties falls back to bus_print_property
      (bus-print-properties.c 377 / 391). With --value only the value is
      printed (bus_print_property_value, bus-print-properties.c 16 / 27); an
      empty value is printed as an empty line or not at all, which reads the
      same after command substitution.
  R2  String properties (D-Bus `s`): printed verbatim (bus-print-properties.c
      64 / 75); a value containing a newline is printed as "[unprintable]"
      (77 / 88), so a multi-line value never comes from this path.
  R3  Boolean properties (`b`): yes_no(b) (bus-print-properties.c 93 / 104),
      which is "yes" or "no" (string-util-fundamental.h 73 / 111).
  R4  UMask (`u`): printed with "%04o" (bus-print-properties.c 201 / 213), so
      0077 renders as "0077".
  R5  uint64 `…USec` properties: rendered as a timestamp only for names ending
      in "Timestamp" and for NextElapseUSecRealtime, LastTriggerUSec, TimeUSec,
      RTCTimeUSec (bus-print-properties.c 107 / 19 bus_property_is_timestamp;
      systemctl's own uint64 branch handles only those, systemctl-show.c
      1096 / 1216). Every other name containing "USec" is FORMAT_TIMESPAN(u, 0)
      (bus-print-properties.c 112-113 / 124-125). format_timespan prints "0"
      for zero (time-util.c 554 / 547), joins units with single spaces
      (598 / 591; table "h" 536 / 529, "min" 537 / 530), and with accuracy 0
      prints a sub-minute remainder in microsecond dot notation (583 / 576),
      e.g. 20 min 1.2 s → "20min 1.200000s"; 6 h 15 min → "6h 15min".
  R6  String arrays (`as`): each element through shell_maybe_quote(str, 0),
      joined by single spaces (bus-print-properties.c 242, 253, 262 /
      254, 265, 274). shell_maybe_quote returns a string unchanged unless it
      holds whitespace, a control character or one of SHELL_NEED_QUOTES
      (escape.c 510 / 552; escape.h 22 / 15); unit names here hold none. An
      empty array prints nothing.
  R7  TimersMonotonic (`a(stt)`): one line per entry,
      "{ <base>=<FORMAT_TIMESPAN(value)> ; next_elapse=<FORMAT_TIMESPAN(next)> }"
      (systemctl-show.c 1330-1351, format at 1340 / 1471, format at 1481). The
      base is timer_base_to_usec_string (dbus-timer.c 38 / 40; timer.c
      1022 / 1029): "OnActiveSec" (timer.c 1012 / 1019) with Sec→USec, i.e.
      "OnActiveUSec"; likewise "OnBootUSec". No entry prints nothing.
  R8  TimersCalendar (`a(sst)`): one line per entry,
      "{ <base>=<spec> ; next_elapse=<FORMAT_TIMESTAMP_STYLE(next)> }"
      (systemctl-show.c 1353 / 1494). No entry prints nothing.
  R9  ExecStart (`a(sasbttttuii)`): one line per command,
      "{ path=%s ; argv[]=%s ; ignore_errors=%s ; start_time=[%s] ;
      stop_time=[%s] ; pid=%i ; code=%s ; status=%i%s%s }" with argv joined by
      single spaces and ignore_errors from yes_no (systemctl-show.c 1374,
      1386, 1409 / 1515, 1527, 1550).
  R10 LastTriggerUSecMonotonic (`t`): created by
      BUS_PROPERTY_DUAL_TIMESTAMP("LastTriggerUSec", …) (dbus-timer.c 113 / 115;
      bus-get-properties.h 99 / 103), which also defines
      "LastTriggerUSecMonotonic"; it is not a timestamp name, so R5 renders it
      as a time span. It is assigned only when the timer elapses
      (timer_enter_running, timer.c 621 / 650); v255 also clears it on every
      start (timer.c 651); units are zero-allocated (unit_new, unit.c 99 / 100)
      and timer_init does not set it (v259 timer.c 38-47). A never-fired timer
      therefore renders "0".
  R11 Property types and getters. dbus-execute.c: Environment `as` 947 / 1223,
      UMask `u` 951 / 1227, WorkingDirectory `s` 984 / 1260 (getter 354 / 365:
      the plain path unless the home directory or missing-ok form is set),
      StandardInput/Output/Error `s` 1011, 1014, 1016 / 1287, 1290, 1292,
      User/Group `s` 1038-1039 / 1314-1315, PrivateTmp `b` 1057 / 1334 (v259
      getter 1046: true for every value but "no"), PrivateNetwork `b`
      1064 / 1343, ProtectHome/ProtectSystem `s` 1068-1069 / 1349-1350,
      NoNewPrivileges `b` 1077 / 1358. dbus-service.c: Type `s` 322 / 326,
      Restart `s` 324 / 328, RemainAfterExit `b` 343 / 347, ExecStart
      373 / 381. dbus-kill.c: KillMode `s` 28 / 29. dbus-unit.c: After `as`
      869 / 918, Description `s` 884 / 934, FragmentPath `s` 890 / 940,
      UnitFileState `s` 893 / 943. dbus-timer.c: Unit `s` 106 / 108,
      TimersMonotonic 107 / 109, TimersCalendar 108 / 110, Persistent `b`
      118 / 121, RemainAfterElapse `b` 120 / 123.
  R12 Enum string values: StandardInput "null" (execute.c 2649 / 3042),
      StandardOutput/Error "journal" (2667 / 3060), KillMode "control-group"
      (kill.c 39 / 42), ProtectHome "yes" (namespace.c 3011 / 3968),
      ProtectSystem "strict" (3022 / 3987), Restart "no" (service.c
      4979 / 5801), Type "oneshot" (5000 / 5823), UnitFileState "disabled"
      (install.c 3733 / 3860).
  R13 InvocationID (`ay`, dbus-unit.c 940 / 991, bus_property_get_id128): a
      byte array, printed as two lowercase hex digits per byte with nothing
      between them (bus-print-properties.c 280-300 / 292-312), i.e. 32 hex
      characters for the 16-byte id.
  R14 ActiveState and SubState (`s`, dbus-unit.c 887, 889 / 937, 939): the
      unit's active state through unit_active_state_to_string (getter
      dbus-unit.c 37 / 44) and, for a service, its state through
      service_sub_state_to_string (service.c 3396-3399 / 3801-3804, wired at
      5117 / 5945). A oneshot service with RemainAfterExit=yes that ran to
      completion is "active" (UNIT_ACTIVE, unit-def.c 108 / 103) and "exited"
      (SERVICE_EXITED, unit-def.c 197 / 224); both are printed by R2.
  R15 ExecMainStatus (`i`): defined by BUS_EXEC_STATUS_VTABLE("ExecMain", …)
      (dbus-service.c 368 / 376; dbus-execute.h 14 / 15, prefix "Status",
      type "i"). systemctl's own int32 branch handles only names ending in
      ActionExitStatus and NUMAPolicy (systemctl-show.c 1070, 1083 /
      1190, 1203), so the generic printer prints it with "%"PRIi32
      (bus-print-properties.c 219-226 / 231-238): exit status 0 → "0".
  R16 Result (`s`, dbus-service.c 356 / 362, an enum getter): "success" for
      SERVICE_SUCCESS (service.c 5051 / 5876), printed by R2.
  R17 NRestarts (`u`, dbus-service.c 363 / 370): not a UMask, Mode, UID or
      GID name, so printed with "%"PRIu32 (bus-print-properties.c 214 / 226):
      no restart → "0".
  R18 systemd-run's own timer property (E3J9E Stage B). transient_timer_set_properties
      (run.c 977-992 / 1701-1716) first applies the --timer-property= assignments
      (transient_unit_set_properties(m, UNIT_TIMER, arg_timer_property)) and then
      unconditionally appends RemainAfterElapse=false (run.c 987 / 1711, "Automatically
      clean up our transient timers"); --remain-after-exit is not consulted there.
      A timer created by systemd-run therefore renders RemainAfterElapse=no (R3),
      whatever --timer-property= requested; a unit-file timer renders its file value.
      Local corroboration: the static disassembly (not executed) of Ubuntu systemd
      255.4-1ubuntu8.17 /usr/bin/systemd-run (sha256
      dbc8b988a849d5c9d7ef2de7068a6f107021bc6c11e0d7864c73f373eef726a7) appends it
      directly after the timer properties. Host corroboration, a class only: E3J9E
      Stage A G6 (Hotel-Echo, systemd 259) recorded tmr_remainafterelapse=differs
      for a systemd-run timer.
  R19 An elapsed transient timer (E3J9E Stage B). A RUNNING timer (UNIT_ACTIVE,
      state table timer.c 27-33 / 28-34) stays RUNNING until its unit becomes
      inactive or failed (timer_trigger_notify, timer.c 774-811 / 795-832); a
      oneshot RemainAfterExit=yes service that exited 0 stays active, so collect
      finds the timer. When the unit fails, the timer re-enters waiting and, with no
      further elapse and RemainAfterElapse=no, goes dead (timer_enter_elapsed,
      timer.c 332-347 / 334-349; leave_around is set only by OnUnitActive= and
      OnUnitInactive= bases, timer.c 453-465 / 483-495). Whether that dead transient
      timer is then garbage-collected while its failed service stays loaded is
      decided by the manager's GC sweep, which is not in the reviewed files
      (unit_may_gc, unit.c 433 / 418, admits an inactive timer; timer.c defines no
      may_gc): NOT established; the harness tests both outcomes.

Normalization (applies to every fixture)
  Generated identifiers are placeholders: @STEM@ (unit stem), @ARMED@ (armed
  boot id), @TRANSIENT@ / @UNITDIR@ (unit directories), @SVC@ (service
  identity), @WRAPPER@ (credential wrapper path), @INVOCATION@ (the unit's
  32-hex invocation id, R13; the harness substitutes its fake run's id). The one time-dependent field
  of TimersMonotonic, next_elapse, is the fixed text "1h 2min 3.500000s" (the
  R5 form of an arbitrary value; the launcher only rejects "{", "}" and ";" in
  it). In ExecStart the fields after "ignore_errors=" (start_time, stop_time,
  pid, code, status) are normalized and not established here; the launcher
  compares only the text before " ; ignore_errors=". In After only the
  presence of network-online.target is asserted; the other entries are
  normalized. A fixture never contains credential values, key material,
  tokens, passwords, URLs, hostnames, IP addresses, journal messages,
  invocation ids, boot ids, pids or captured timestamps; the harness lint
  enforces this on every file below.

Coverage groups (approved plan §9): (1) transient service before firing,
(2) transient timer before firing, (3) fired service and timer states that
collect depends on (the provider-fired pair: InvocationID, ActiveState,
SubState, ExecMainStatus, Result, NRestarts, the fixed attestation properties
and a non-zero LastTriggerUSecMonotonic; plus the fired-value variant), (4) persistent boot service and timer, (5) query failure — injected by
the harness (status and stderr), not a fixture, (6) empty, multi-line,
malformed, semantically different and version-variant output. Sentinel, empty
and multi-line values that the harness writes for single properties are
synthetic test inputs, not fixtures.

